README-SHELLS -- five shells, and which one to takeIf you are copying part of this collection onto your own media you want ONEshell, and probably two: one to type at and one for programs to shell outthrough.  This file exists so you do not have to install all five to find outhow they differ.Short answer: take `bash' to type at, and `ksh' as well if anything you useshells out -- which is more programs than you would guess.  Take `sh' aloneonly if you need the smallest real Bourne shell and will not run anythingthat calls system().  `gshell' and `mshell' are not command shells at all;they are full-screen menus, and they are worth having for that.THE FIVE--------  file      bytes   needs   module   source            docs                     cio     name  ------------------------------------------------------------------------  bash     242604    no     bash     -                 DOC/bash (3 files)  sh        77306    no     sh       -                 DOC/sh (4 files)  ksh      118554    YES    ksh      SRC/pdksh (171)   DOC/ksh, DOC/pdksh,                                                       DOC/README-KSH  gshell    23114    no     gshell   SRC/eff_gshell    DOC/gshell (4 files)  mshell     7228    YES    mshell   -                 -  bash    GNU Bourne-Again Shell 1.12 -- this disk's own, and what SYS/login          hands you  sh      Bourne shell v7.5, Martin Gregorie's sh75 package  ksh     pdksh, the public-domain Korn shell  gshell  GSHELL V1.1, Uwe Simon 1988 -- a menu  mshell  a menu shell driven by a menu fileTHE ONE THING THAT DECIDES IT: WHAT $SHELL HAS TO DO----------------------------------------------------Programs that shell out -- TeX's `tex' and `latex' wrappers, `eo', RCS's`ci' -- reach this C library's system(), and it forks $SHELL WITH THE WHOLECOMMAND LINE AS ONE ARGUMENT.  That is how Microware's own shell is invokedand it is what these programs were built against.Measured 2026-08-31, handing each shell the single argument`/dd/CMDS/wc -c /dd/SYS/motd':  ksh      40 /dd/SYS/motd                      it parses it.  WORKS  bash     "/dd/CMDS/wc: cannot execute binary file"           -- bash reads the argument as a SCRIPT FILENAME.  It has -c and           `bash -c '...'` works perfectly; system() never sends -c  sh       "/dd/CMDS/wc: file not found"           -- sh cannot fork an absolute pathname here, which is its own           long-standing limitation on this disk  gshell   opens its menu with `Directory : /dd/CMDS/wc -c /dd/SYS/motd'  mshell   "Too many items in /dd/CMDS/wc -c /dd/SYS/motd (> 10)"So SYS/login sets `SHELL=$ROOT/CMDS/ksh', and that one line is what makes`tex' and `latex' work at all: without it they do nothing and return 221;with it they typeset a document.  `eo' goes from `can't execute /dd/bash' torunning its command on every line of a file.The cost is four lines of `F$SetSys: unimplemented 03D8' from os9exec everytime ksh starts.  They are noise from the emulator, not an error, and theyappear only when something shells out.RCS is the one this does NOT rescue.  `ci' builds `diff -n a b >-tmpfile',and `>-' is Microware shell redirection that no shell here understands, so asecond revision still cannot be checked in.  See DOC/INDEX under `ci'.bash -- the one to type at--------------------------  GNU Bourne-Again Shell 1.12.  Command history, job control, functions,  and scripts that come across from Unix unchanged.  It is the shell  SYS/login exec's, and /dd/.bashrc is read because login points HOME at  this disk.  TWO OF ITS BUILT-INS ARE REPLACED IN .bashrc AND MUST STAY REPLACED.  bash's own `pwd' HANGS the shell and, where /dd is not an RBF device, its  `cd' aborts with a bus error: getwd() walks `..' upward looking for a  single root, and OS-9 has one root per device, so the walk never ends.  /dd/.bashrc replaces both with functions that track the path as a string.  A consequence worth knowing before you write a test: bash's `cd' does not  move the OS-9 DATA directory.  `cd somewhere; prog' runs prog with the  data directory wherever it already was, so a program that opens a file by  bare name will not find it where you think.  Costs: the largest here by a factor of two, and it cannot serve as $SHELL.sh -- the small real Bourne shell----------------------------------  Bourne shell v7.5 from Martin Gregorie's sh75 package -- the same package  `cat', `basename' and `dirname' came from.  A third of bash's size, needs  no Microware module, and it has a real `chd': `pwd' inside it reports a  directory that actually moved, which bash cannot do.  Costs, and they are severe here: it CANNOT FORK A PROGRAM BY ABSOLUTE  PATHNAME under os9exec.  `sh -c '/dd/CMDS/wc -c file'` answers `file not  found' for a file that plainly exists, with PATH set and `chx' done.  So  it cannot launch most of this collection, and it cannot be $SHELL.  Take it if you want the smallest genuine Bourne shell and a working `chd',  and you will run programs by short name from a directory on PATH.ksh -- the one programs need-----------------------------  pdksh, the public-domain Korn shell, whole source in SRC/pdksh (171 files)  and its manual page in DOC/ksh.  It is the only shell here that takes a command line as a single argument,  which is what makes it the right $SHELL.  `ksh -c '<commands>'` works too.  Its INTERACTIVE loop depends on the emulator: it reads a command line with  read(fd, buf, 256), and an os9exec whose I$Read waited for the full count  rather than returning at the end-of-record character never saw a typed  command.  With that corrected it is a full shell -- prompt, for loops,  variables, forking.  DOC/README-KSH is the whole story.  Costs: it needs Microware's `cio', which ships here with Microware's  permission but which you may not have on your own machine.  And it makes  os9exec print four `F$SetSys: unimplemented' lines when it starts.gshell and mshell -- menus, not shells---------------------------------------  Neither reads commands from a keyboard the way the other three do.  `gshell' draws a full-screen file browser: a lettered list of the current  directory, `+' and `-' to page, `.' to change directory, a letter to run  a file and its lower-case twin to run it with parameters.  Three  front-ends on this disk drive the same engine -- `assembler', `compiler'  and `editor' -- and each is that menu pointed at one job.  Uwe Simon,  1988; source in SRC/eff_gshell, help text in DOC/gshell.  `mshell' is smaller still and reads a MENU FILE named on its command  line, saying `Could not open <name> (menufile)' without one.  Both want  TERM set, as every full-screen program here does.  Take one of these if you want to hand somebody a machine that runs four  things and nothing else.  Do not take one expecting to type at it.CHOOSING--------  You want a shell to work in                            bash  Something you use shells out (TeX, eo, RCS, make)      ksh, as $SHELL  You want the smallest real Bourne shell                sh  You want a real `chd' that moves the data directory    sh  You have no cio and cannot get one                     bash or sh  You want a menu for somebody else to use               gshell  If you take exactly one file: `bash'.  If you take two, `bash' and `ksh',  and set SHELL to the ksh -- SYS/login on this disk shows the line.